classes.rule:
A bare verb form stands only where English allows one:
- opening a command;
- after a modal, "do" or "to";
- after a plural subject or I/you/we/they;
- after the object of let/make/have/help/see/watch/hear/feel.

Right after a form of "be", a bare form makes no verb group, since "be" takes only an -ing or past form. Right after the object of any other verb (leave, keep, turn, spin), a word without an -s/-ed/-ing ending says what the object becomes or is. It is a clause modifier, or a dependent of a nominal that ends a relation, never a verb and never the head.
